## 🚀 Project AURA – “Greater‑Than” Utility  

**Goal** – Provide a tiny, production‑ready C++ library that determines the greater of two (or more) numbers, with clean architecture, documentation, and unit tests.

---  

### 📁 Directory Layout
```
greater_than/
│─ include/
│   └─ greater_than.hpp      # public API
│─ src/
│   └─ greater_than.cpp      # implementation
│─ tests/
│   └─ greater_than_test.cpp # unit tests (GoogleTest)
│─ CMakeLists.txt            # build script
│─ README.md                 # (this file)
```

---  

### 🧩 Public API – `greater_than.hpp`
```cpp
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace aura {

/**
 * @brief Returns the greatest element among two values.
 *
 * @tparam T Comparable type (supports operator<)
 * @param a First value
 * @param b Second value
 * @return const T& Reference to the larger value
 */
template <typename T>
inline const T& max_of_two(const T& a, const T& b) {
    return (a < b) ? b : a;
}

/**
 * @brief Returns the greatest element in a container.
 *
 * @tparam T Container element type (must be comparable)
 * @param values Container (e.g., std::vector, std::array)
 * @return const T& Reference to the largest element
 * @throws std::invalid_argument if the container is empty
 */
template <typename Container>
inline const typename Container::value_type& max_of_many(const Container& values) {
    if (values.empty())
        throw std::invalid_argument("Container must contain at least one element");
    return *std::max_element(values.begin(), values.end());
}

} // namespace aura
```

*Key points*  
- Header‑only → no linking hassles.  
- `constexpr`‑friendly (inline) for zero‑overhead.  
- Throws a clear exception for empty containers.

